@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+# Collector transport qualification
+
+The collector uses JVM `javax.net.ssl.trustStore` configuration for HTTPS and JMX chain trust. HTTPS verifies the requested DNS/IP identity and allows TLS 1.2/1.3. SSH reads the file named by `-Dhertzbeat.ssh.knownHosts=/run/hertzbeat/known_hosts` (default: `$user.home/.ssh/known_hosts`). Provision trusted OpenSSH entries, including `[hostname]:port` for nondefault ports; missing, unknown and changed keys are rejected. SFTP retains SHA256 fingerprint pinning and rejects the insecure opt-out.
+
+Secured JMX requires `ssl=true` and an additional JVM security properties file loaded **at JVM startup**:
+
+```properties
+ssl.SocketFactory.provider=org.apache.hertzbeat.collector.collect.common.http.VerifiedSslSocketFactory
+```
+
+Pass `-Djava.security.properties=/run/hertzbeat/verified-tls.security` (one `=`, appending to platform security properties). This provider ensures the JDK SSL RMI client verifies certificate names on both registry and exported RMI sockets. Configure the server with TLS on its registry and exported JMX endpoint, its advertised hostname matching certificate SANs, and authentication. The connector accepts standard JDK SSL RMI stubs and rejects plaintext or custom socket factories before sending credentials to an RMIServer; it validates the returned RMIConnection stub too. Provider absence fails before connection. The standard `sun.rmi.server.UnicastRef2` implementation name is checked to reject plaintext stubs without reflective access to JDK internals; alternate JMX/RMI implementations require separate qualification and are deliberately rejected. Trust/key store or provider changes require a collector restart because JSSE and RMI cache factories/connections.
+
+Run the actual local fixture probes with JDK 21 or later and cached Maven dependencies:
+
+```sh
+python3 hertzbeat-collector/security-tests/run_transport_probes.py
+```
+
+The script uses `MAVEN_REPO` (default `~/.m2/repository`) and falls back to the isolated integration cache `/tmp/lme-hertzbeat-m2`. It compiles the actual CommonHttpClient, CommonSshClient, VerifiedSslSocketFactory and VerifiedJmxConnector sources. It creates synthetic two-day certificates/keys under a temporary directory and only connects to local fixture endpoints. No certificate/private key survives the run. The checks are HTTPS valid/wrong SAN/untrusted issuer; genuine JMX MBean reads with valid credentials, bad credentials, wrong SAN/untrusted issuer on each of registry and exported RMI stub, plaintext stub downgrade, and missing provider; SSH valid/changed/unknown/missing known-host keys. These probes are separate from the Maven/JUnit suite because each JSSE provider configuration needs its own JVM.
+
+Observed baseline: the original CommonHttpClient accepted a trusted certificate for the wrong SAN (`AssertionError: HTTPS wrong-san accepted=true`). Modified source passes all listed probes. Maven focused tests additionally cover the existing HTTP/SSH/JMX/SFTP collector paths. These fixtures do not qualify a packaged Spring Boot launch or a deployed remote service; deployment still must mount trusted material and the provider configuration correctly.
