+WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.
+See the License for the specific language governing permissions and
+limitations under the License.
+-->
+
+# Native metric export
+
+This optional path captures collector samples synchronously in `DataStorageDispatch`
+before history writers can consume or release Arrow data. OTLP imports use a separate
+storage path and do not enter this exporter. `CollectRep` numeric observations lack
+counter temporality metadata, so they are emitted as OTLP gauges with original source
+time, numeric values, native units, and every schema-declared row dimension.
+
+```yaml
+hertzbeat:
+  native-export:
+    enabled: false
+    directory: /var/lib/hertzbeat/native-metrics
+    endpoint: https://FIXED-PROFILE-GATEWAY/v1/metrics
+    sinks: elastic,hertzbeat
+    host-id: COLLECTOR-SOURCE-ID
+    host-name: COLLECTOR-SOURCE-NAME
+    allowed-labels: region
+    auth-header-file: /run/secrets/native-export-authorization
+    auth-header-map-file: /run/secrets/hertzbeat/native-source-authorization.json
+    endpoint-map-file: /run/secrets/hertzbeat/native-source-endpoints.json
+    max-bytes: 268435456
+    reserve-bytes: 2147483648
+```
+
+Provision the directory for only the HertzBeat owner. Use the JVM trust store for the
+gateway CA: the HTTP client verifies certificate chains and hostnames and never follows
+redirects. The authorization file contains one complete Authorization header value;
+it is reread for each request and is never persisted with samples or included in logs.
+Use the two maps together for multiple sources. Each JSON object maps an exact canonical
+resource `host.name` (for example `physical:graph` or `guest:trixie`) to an absolute
+authorization-file path or a verified HTTPS OTLP endpoint respectively. Missing entries,
+ambiguous source identities, and relative credential paths fail admission. Without maps,
+the single endpoint/authorization-file fallback permits only the configured `host-name`.
+Each pending record snapshots its URL, authorization-file reference and sinks, preserving
+old obligations during map/profile changes. Preserve referenced files until all pending
+obligations complete; credential contents may rotate at the same path. Monitor labels
+`host.id` and `host.name` override collector defaults for remote monitored targets;
+reconciliation must set both. Other custom labels require the explicit allowlist.
+
+## Durable handoff and verification
+
+Admission writes a random UUID `.pending` record, fsyncs its contents, atomically renames
+it, and fsyncs the directory. The UUID is solely an outbox/transport occurrence ID and
+never appears in metric labels. Requests and records are bounded to 1 MiB; queue
+admission stops at its byte limit or the 2 GiB filesystem reserve. Such rejected samples
+are collection gaps: the dispatcher reports failure, the exporter increments
+`getAdmissionFailures()`, and ordinary local history processing continues.
+
+HTTP 200 with full OTLP acceptance only renames the record to `.accepted`. It remains
+inside the bounded outbox until **every admission-time sink** verifies retrieved
+content. No time-based purge removes unverified samples. Round-robin batches permit
+other records to progress during transport failures or poisoned records.
+The exporter exposes retained-byte, transport-failure, readback-failure, and unreadable
+record counters through its bean; the deployment's monitoring adapter must collect them.
+
+The external destination query adapter reads immutable `UUID.accepted` files. Their
+binary format is Java `DataOutputStream`: int32 big-endian version `2`; `writeUTF`
+endpoint (uint16 byte length followed by Java modified UTF-8); `writeUTF` absolute
+authorization-file reference (never the credential contents); int32 sink count;
+one `writeUTF` sink per obligation; int32 payload length; UTF-8 OTLP JSON payload.
+The supported sink lists are `elastic` and `elastic,hertzbeat`.
+The reader also accepts legacy version `1`, which omitted the authorization-file
+reference. Those records may use the single credential fallback only for the exact
+configured source; unknown legacy sources remain pending for explicit recovery.
+
+For each successfully queried sink the adapter atomically publishes:
+
+```json
+{"id":"UUID","sink":"elastic","payload":{"resourceMetrics":[]}}
+```
+
+The filename is `readbacks/UUID.elastic.json` (or `.hertzbeat.json`). The real payload
+must contain the complete OTLP content reconstructed from retrieved destination values;
+the empty array above only illustrates the schema. Mount accepted records read-only for
+the adapter and grant it write access only to `readbacks/`. The exporter compares JSON
+content while ignoring object and native gauge array ordering; scalar types and duplicate
+multiplicity remain significant. A copied sender hash never satisfies verification.
+
+The pinned Elasticsearch ECS exporter drops unit/type/scope envelope metadata. Each
+point therefore also carries `lme.metric.unit`, `lme.metric.type=gauge`, and
+`lme.scope.name=org.apache.hertzbeat.native` attributes for reconstruction from queried
+documents. Reconstruct `asDouble` as a floating JSON number and `timeUnixNano` as a string.
+Successful per-sink receipts are fsynced; only all verified sinks permit sample retirement.
+
+Focused tests cover immutable Arrow lifetime, source time and dimensions, secret-label
+minimization, invalid numbers, restart recovery, independent sink verification, quota
+and reserve rejection, content mismatch, poisoning, fair retries and secure endpoint
+validation. Live gateway TLS, native sink mapping and end-to-end destination readback
+remain deployment acceptance requirements.
